"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D2_AFRIKA_AMERIKA:-0374-01-01:0":{"b": ["Burning of the first plaza of San Martin Tilcajete by Monte Alban: the plaza is burned (Monte Alban attack)", "718e1374"], "d": ["At the end of Early Monte Alban I, the first plaza of the rival center Tilcajete was burned; afterwards a new plaza was built on a more defensible ridge. Its palace (Structure 7) burned with calibrated dates in the range 100 BCE-140 CE, and its temple (Structure 16) in the range 165 BCE-155 CE.", "bf888151"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D2_AFRIKA_AMERIKA:-0659-01-01:0":{"b": ["San Jose Mogote: burned temple (Structure 28): the Structure 28 temple burns", "c677f22f"], "d": ["In the middle of the Rosario phase the main temple of San Jose Mogote (Structure 28) was burned in an attack; Structure 37 (840-400 BCE) was built in its place.", "3c142b66"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D2_AFRIKA_AMERIKA:-0999-01-01:0":{"b": ["Ceibal (Seibal, Pasion, Guatemala): Real 1 phase: first sedentary community", "dd836649"], "d": ["In the Middle Preclassic, the first formal ceremonial complex (E-Group) together with pottery, ~950 BCE; among the earliest examples of monumental architecture in the Maya lowlands in the Pasion region. Early Middle Preclassic Real 1 (1000-850), Real 2 (850-800), Real 3 (800-700); Late Middle Preclassic Escoba 1 (700-600), 2 (600-450), 3 (450-350).", "a82b67e5"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D2_AFRIKA_AMERIKA:-2549-01-01:0":{"b": ["Early Kerma (Kerma Ancien): Early Kerma begins", "20c8acd2"], "d": ["The first phase of the Kerma culture (Early Kerma, the phase distinguished by Honegger as Early Kerma 0, before CE28); this phase lies between Pre-Kerma and Middle Kerma.", "a023541c"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D2_AFRIKA_AMERIKA:-2799-01-01:0":{"b": ["Kotosh Religious Tradition (Kotosh, Peru): the Kotosh Religious Tradition begins", "3a89f318"], "d": ["A religious tradition recognized by a round structure + central hearth + underground ventilation duct; the type site is Kotosh (Huanuco). La Seductora is a small family shrine.", "ceb0e477"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D2_AFRIKA_AMERIKA:-2834-01-01:0":{"b": ["Gobero (Tenere circle, Niger): the youngest Tenerean burial", "c42fb27f"], "d": ["A Saharan lakeside necropolis with two distinct settlement phases in the Paleo-lake: Early Holocene (Kiffian) hunter-fisher-gatherers, around ~7500 BCE the oldest known cemetery in the Sahara; an arid interval (6200-5200 BCE); then Tenerean herders (5200-2500 BCE).", "95283ef9"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D2_AFRIKA_AMERIKA:-3001-01-01:0":{"b": ["Lothagam North Pillar Site (Lake Turkana, Kenya): use of the monumental cemetery platform begins", "12c308db"], "d": ["The oldest and largest monumental cemetery in East Africa: a burial cavity for >=580 people (119.5 m2), a stone-ringed platform; the first herders of the Turkana basin.", "4608bf05"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D2_AFRIKA_AMERIKA:-3184-01-01:0":{"b": ["Tehuacan Valley, San Marcos Cave - the oldest maize (partial domesticate): the oldest maize (San Marcos, Tehuacan)", "23af2d5a"], "d": ["The oldest of the 3 cobs from San Marcos cave, re-excavated in 2012, are 5,300-4,970 cal BP; this corresponds to the earliest Tehuacan cultural phase.", "c75bbbbe"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D2_AFRIKA_AMERIKA:-3749-01-01:0":{"b": ["Los Morteros (Chao Valley, Peru) - the oldest adobe monumental architecture: the oldest adobe monumental architecture in the Americas", "1ddb48c2"], "d": ["The oldest known adobe monumental structure on the northern Peruvian coast (Chao Valley); abstract: 'invention of adobe architecture before 5,100 calendar years B.P.' Linked to El Nino rains. This article gives no data for Sechin Alto/Sechin Bajo.", "16c7015b"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D2_AFRIKA_AMERIKA:-7699-01-01:0":{"b": ["Gobero (Tenere circle, Niger): Early Holocene (Kiffian) occupation begins", "b78f5299"], "d": ["A Saharan lakeside necropolis with two distinct settlement phases in the Paleo-lake: Early Holocene (Kiffian) hunter-fisher-gatherers, around ~7500 BCE the oldest known cemetery in the Sahara; an arid interval (6200-5200 BCE); then Tenerean herders (5200-2500 BCE).", "95283ef9"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D2_ANADOLU_EGE:-0546-01-01:0":{"b": ["Sardis — the Persian capture: the Persian king Cyrus takes Sardis (end of Lydia)", "d37e3614"], "d": ["Sardis, the capital of Lydia, fell to the Persian Cyrus; burned walls and houses were found in the destruction layer, and coins of the time of Croesus beneath the destruction level.", "4ec7b32f"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D2_ANADOLU_EGE:-0739-01-01:0":{"b": ["Gordion — Tumulus MM (Midas Mound): Midas Tumulus (MM): felling year of the trees of the burial chamber (dendrochronology)", "e017e788"], "d": ["The largest Phrygian tumulus at Gordion; the felling year is dated by tree rings of the inner chamber timber (juniper) (the bark year is present), the felling ≈ the construction year of the tumulus.", "8ba1386d"]},
